    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "First U.S.- Soviet Joint Space Mission\nJuly 15, 1975 • Moscow, U.S.S.R.\nECOP 12K\nАПОЛЛОН СОЮЗ 1975\n(почтА 5 СССР\n15078415\nМОСКВ\nA",
      "International Tributes to the History of Flight\nFirst U.S.- Soviet Joint Space Mission\nJust after 8:00 a.m. on July 15, 1975, a Vostok rocket\nlowed on July 24.\nlifted the Soviet spacecraft Soyuz XIX into orbit with\nCosmonauts Alexei Leonov and Valeriy Kubasov on\nThe full-color cachet depicts the two spacecraft\nboard. At 3:50 p.m. that same day, an American Apollo\ngetting ready for the link-up. Because it carried more\nspacecraft manned by Thomas Stafford, Vance Brand\nfuel, Apollo served as the pursuit vehicle and exe-\nand Donald Slayton was boosted skyward atop a Sat-\ncuted the approach maneuvers as it neared Soyuz.\nurn V rocket. Thus began the first U.S.-Soviet joint\nThe two crafts were then linked by a docking module\n\"tunnel.\"\nspace mission.\nTraveling at more than 17,000 miles per hour, the\nMint Stamp: The mint stamp on this Cover was issued\ntwo crafts rendezvoused in space and maneuvered\nby the USSR in 1975. It pictures the Soyuz XIX and\ninto the docking position on July 17. They were over\nApollo craft in space.\nthe Atlantic when the triumphant report came, \"Con-\nPostmark: This Cover was postmarked in Moscow,\ntact — we have succeeded.\" For two days, the crews\nUSSR on July 15, 1984, the exact anniversary of the\nmoved back and forth between Apollo and Soyuz.\nstart of the first U.S.-Soviet joint manned space\nSoyuz returned to Earth on July 21 and Apollo fol-\nmission.\n© Postal Commemorative Society\nPrinted in U.S.A.\n2015.0560.047"
    ],
